Overlay patch 0049: THE PARAMETRIC PHYSICAL SHIELD (VR R5, VR-DONOR-MAP 8,
donor `VrShield.cpp` plus its three widened vanilla gates).

R4 shipped the FACING VETO (overlay 0046) and nothing else of the shield: the
quad stayed vanilla's 60x60 square in its vanilla place, and the shield was
raised with R. This patch is the rest of the donor's design.

THE ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND FIRST, because it is not what the brief for this
round assumed and it changes what "raise your shield" means:

  THE DONOR HAS NO RAISE GESTURE. There is no height threshold, no forearm-vs-
  chest dot product, no orientation test, nowhere in its 260 lines. The shield
  is registered EVERY FRAME wherever the off-hand controller is, and "hold it up
  and you block, hand at your side and you get hit" is EMERGENT from two things:
  the quad IS the visible shield at the tracked hand, so an attack that misses
  it misses it; and the 65-degree facing cone rejects a shield that is not
  pointing at the attacker. We ship that, and we did not invent a gesture the
  donor does not have. If a discrete raise is ever wanted it is new design, not
  a port, and it is listed as such in docs/VR-R5-NOTES.md.

  What DOES change from R4 is that the shield no longer needs R at all: it is up
  because your arm is up, and the vanilla stance flag PLAYER_STATE1_SHIELDING is
  never set by any of this.

(A) THE HELD PREDICATE. `Player_VrShieldHeld` keys on `leftHandType` -- the
SWORD's visibility -- rather than on the logical held item, exactly as the donor
does after its own commit df594221 rewrote it. leftHandType is the OUTPUT of
Player_SetModels' central derivation, so it IS what the player can see; keying
on it means the shield leaves the arm the same frame the sword leaves the hand,
including the visual-only stow cases (itemAction = -1) where heldItemAction
still claims the sword. The donor's earlier heldItemAction version needed a
deny-list and had to chase cases one at a time. The Deku stick is the one
special case, and it is the donor's: the stick has no hand model of its own and
renders over a closed fist, so LH_CLOSED counts as "visibly in hand" only while
the held action really is the stick.

Suspended in the hands-off states the donor lists: climbing a ladder, climbing
or hanging off a ledge, in water, on horseback, carrying an actor, dead. Plus
ours: no VR, not first person, motion hands off, the SHIELD hand not tracked.

(B) THE THREE WIDENED GATES.

  1. z_player_lib.c Player_SetModelsForHoldingShield -- the shield MODEL goes
     into the off hand and comes off Link's back. Vanilla tests
     PLAYER_STATE1_SHIELDING; we test that OR Player_VrShieldHeld.

     And the non-obvious second half, which the donor learned the hard way and
     documents at length: vanilla's unconditional `this->itemAction = -1` at the
     bottom becomes STANCE-ONLY. That -1 is the "models diverged" marker, and
     Player_UpdateItems only processes item buttons while
     (itemAction == heldItemAction || SHIELDING). In the stance, the flag keeps
     buttons alive despite the -1. Held physically FULL TIME with no flag, the
     -1 would close that gate FOREVER: B could never draw the sword and C could
     never use an item. This is the single most breakable line in the patch.

  2. z_player_lib.c Player_UpdateShieldCollider -- whether the shield quad is
     registered as a collider at all, and (ours, the parametric part) WHICH
     vertices it gets. Same widening, plus: while physically held, the four
     verts come from Player_VrShieldQuadVerts instead of vanilla's quadSrc.

  3. z_player.c func_808382DC's bounce branch -- what a block DOES. While
     physically held we keep the damage negation and the Deku-shield fire burn
     and skip the stance reaction animation and the `linearVelocity = -18`
     shove. The donor's reason, and it is the right one: the player's real arm
     holds the line, so shoving Link backwards contradicts what the player just
     did with their body.

  Plus the two stance ENTRIES the donor suppresses, for a reason that is not
  cosmetic: both of them set the itemAction = -1 marker outside the action
  lifecycle that repairs it. Player_ActionHandler_11 is the R-button crouch
  (the Sense GRIP is bound to R, so every firm squeeze mid-swing crouched Link),
  and func_80834758 is the Z-target upper-body stance.

(C) THE PARAMETRIC QUAD. Built from scratch in R_HAND MODEL space -- the same
space vanilla's quadSrc lives in, and the space the limb matrix that overlay
0042 pins to the controller transforms out of. A symmetric TRAPEZOID (top width,
bottom width, height) so a Hylian taper is expressible, tilted by pitch/yaw/roll
as R = Rz(roll) * Ry(yaw) * Rx(pitch).

  The subtle part, and the donor says it cost them: THE SHIFTS ARE APPLIED IN
  THE COLLIDER'S OWN TILTED FRAME. centre = axW*shiftX + axH*shiftY + axN*shiftZ
  where axW/axH/axN are the rotated axes. Shifting in raw hand axes made "up/
  down" slide sideways at any tilt, and no amount of rotation could fix it
  because the offsets did not rotate with the quad.

  Vertex order is vanilla's zigzag, which Collider_SetQuadVertices expects:
  0 bottom-left, 1 bottom-right, 2 top-left, 3 top-right.

  Nine numbers, the donor's shipped defaults, all live from `vr set`:
  WidthTop 21.2 / WidthBottom 11.9 / Height 18 (FULL dimensions in game units,
  halved into the x100 model space) / ShiftX -1.1 / ShiftY -0.8 / ShiftZ -2.5
  (game units, x100) / Pitch -4 / Yaw 0 / Roll -90 (degrees). NONE of them has
  been seen in a headset by anyone in this program; they are PCVR numbers tuned
  against a different controller in a different hand, and the roll in particular
  is the one most likely to be wrong for a Sense unit.

(D) HOW IT MEETS 0046. 0046's facing veto already tests the same shell state
this predicate wraps -- VR first person, motion hands on, shield hand tracked --
inline, so the two cannot disagree about whether the shield is physical, and
0046 keeps standing alone without a symbol from this patch. The veto stays the
INNER condition exactly as R4 shipped it: a hit must first touch the quad, and
only then is the cone consulted. What changes underneath it is WHERE the quad
is: it is now the parametric trapezoid on the tracked hand rather than vanilla's
square in its stance-era place.

GATING. Every branch is `#ifdef __IOS__` and every one of them reduces to the
vanilla predicate when gSohVRFpActive is 0. The flat window, the 3D panel, the
macOS build and every iPhone build see this file's behaviour byte for byte as
they do today.

